{
  "summary": "The 'terminal_stage' proposal from round 7 is the most pragmatic suggestion yet, but it has two significant blind spots that undermine its claimed simplicity. First, it conflates 'stopping the pipeline' with 'completing the item' — the proposal says 'move to done and return' when terminal_stage is reached, but this skips final-human-approval entirely, which is the user's last checkpoint before work is considered finished. A plan-only board where plans auto-complete to 'done' without human sign-off defeats the purpose of the approval gate that exists precisely to catch agent errors. The terminal_stage logic needs to route to final-human-approval (not done) as the landing stage, but then you need to handle the fact that final-human-approval's 'approve' action currently moves items to 'done' via the standard flow — meaning the UI and server API approval handlers (server/api.go, tui.py move_feature) don't know whether 'approve from final-human-approval' should mean 'done' or 'back into the pipeline for more phases.' Second, and more fundamentally, all three proposals (recipes, gating, terminal_stage) ignore the WebUI and TUI rendering problem. The STAGES list drives directory creation, stage listing in the TUI sidebar (tui.py:2680), the move-to-stage dialog (tui.py:1019-1020), the server API board state endpoint (server_client.py:224), and the Go server's HTML templates. A plan-only board would still show empty 'implementing', 'testing', 'review' columns in both interfaces, creating a confusing UX where most of the board is permanently empty. None of the proposals address filtering the displayed stages per board, which is arguably more important to the user experience than the pipeline execution question. The right critique here is that everyone has been focused on the pipeline orchestration layer while ignoring that the stage list is a UI contract, not just an execution contract — and any real implementation needs to address both simultaneously or the feature will feel half-built."
}
